currently i have a python repo that execute the rule checking for semiconductor chip (mainly AI chip such as broadcom hammer, hellcat, jericot4c, and GUC k2 bga)

- a product is mainly composed of:
 - substrate
  - top view:
    - C4 ball (for die attach)
    - SMD (SMT small components)
    - pin 1
    - fiducial mark
    - 2D barcode
    - die (after die attach)
  - bottom view:
    - BGA balls
    - SMD (SMT small components)
    - pin 1
    - fiducial mark
 - lid (or called ring)
  - top view:
    - 2D barcode
- a product will have multiple drawings
 - SBT : substrate and the components on it
 - lid : lid and the components on it
 - BD : substrate and the components on it for bottom and top, for top sometimes it has lid
 - POD : combined of substrate and lid and the components of them


- currently the python program is mainly to replace human to:
  - check the validity of drawing (consistent component and coordinate across different drawings), example:
    - the difference of position of bga balls across drawing must within tolerance
    - count the number of fiducial mark, make sure the number is 4 and the shape of 3 must be same with another one
  - check the dimension of the components and their coordinate relative to other components to make sure it can be fabricated, example:
    - nearest distance of the fiducial mark vertices point to lid or substrate

- currently the program flow is:
  - recognize the component from the drawing (maybe dxf)
  - parse the dxf into csv
  - match the component with the csv entities
  - use the raw entities (polylines, arc, etc) to compute the rule checking
  - for each rule, we will find the needed components from csv, get the points, and do measurement


